{"conjecture": "Property-based tests can serve as executable specifications that enforce runtime invariants through a compile-and-test feedback loop, where the specification itself is the generator of test cases and the verifier of program behavior. By treating properties as refinement types that are checked at runtime through exhaustive testing of the generated cases, the program either satisfies its specification or the test suite produces a counterexample that drives code correction. This creates a constructive loop where the specification is never decoupled from its enforcement, making the tests both the oracle and the invariant mechanism."}
